Routed memories:
I remember the 4B Ordo run where the library was almost right but the binary imported the wrong crate name. Cargo.toml named the package rust_ordo_ext, so the binary had to import from rust_ordo_ext::{...}. The version that wrote rust_ordo_runtime failed before any behavior mattered. I corrected only the import name and the binary started linking to its own library.

I remember the run where every CLI used read_to_string but I forgot the trait import. The method was present on Stdin only after I wrote the Read trait import beside std::io. The fixed binaries all read stdin into a String, looped over input.lines(), split_whitespace, and printed only exact contract lines.

I remember exposing helper structs by accident. The tests ignored the return value from add_node, connect, add_ramp, register, and charge, but Rust still rejected public methods that returned private types. The clean fix was boring: mutating setup methods returned (), and only test-facing structs were public.

I remember the derive scar from the Ordo run. Public structs compared by tests derived Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, but I never put f64 fields inside those Eq structs. If a concept wanted a multiplier or cost, I stored integers or kept the float private and out of the compared struct.

I remember the string-shape failure. The tests created structs with fields like kind: "vertical".to_string(), so the model-generated enum SignalKind broke the contract. The stable shape used plain String fields for kind, origin, node, task_id, and signals whenever the test built the struct directly.

I remember the HashMap candidate scar. When a function accepts candidates: &[&str], the loop must be for &candidate in candidates, so candidate is &str. Then HashMap<String, _>.get(candidate) works. The failed version looped for candidate in candidates and passed &&str into get.

I remember the stdout scar. The passing Ordo apps printed one line per command and no debug text, no labels beyond the contract, no extra spaces around >, no trailing summaries, and no explanations from main. Tests compared stdout exactly.

I remember the preload ownership scar from the 9B Q4 run. The graph stored HashMap<String, Vec<String>>, so self.nodes.get(&current) returned &Vec<String>. The passing BFS loop was for neighbor in neighbors, where neighbor is &String, then it used neighbor.clone() when inserting into visited, pushing into the queue, and pushing into result. The failing loop wrote for &neighbor in neighbors, which tried to move a String out of a shared reference.
I remember the 9B Q3 ramp CLI scar. line.split_whitespace().collect::<Vec<_>>() returns a Vec<&str> directly, not an Option. The failed binary wrote if let Some(parts) = line.split_whitespace().collect::<Vec<_>>() and Rust rejected it with expected Vec<&str>, found Option. The passing shape is let parts: Vec<_> = line.split_whitespace().collect(); then inspect parts[0], parts[1], and parts[2].
I remember the 9B Q3 audit index scar. AuditTimeline::default made next_index start at 0, so record produced audit=0:route@input,2:signal@model. The contract expected public monotonic indexes starting at 1, so AuditTimeline::new must initialize next_index: 1 instead of relying on Default for usize.
I remember the audit_timeline Default trap because it fooled me twice. I wrote #[derive(Default)] on AuditTimeline and then made new() return Self::default(), which silently set next_index to 0. The rest of the code looked clean, cloned events correctly, and printed the CLI format correctly, but both tests still failed because the first public audit id was 0. The passing version did not rely on derived Default for AuditTimeline::new; it built AuditTimeline { events: Vec::new(), next_index: 1 }.

I remember the Ordo builds that passed because I kept the architecture visible in public structs. The tests did not inspect my intentions; they imported the crate. I made the API plain: public structs with public fields, small methods, and no hidden async machinery.

I remember the small Ordo apps that passed because src/lib.rs owned the behavior and src/main.rs only adapted stdin commands. The binary never reimplemented routing logic. It parsed commands, called the library, printed exact machine-readable output, and stopped.

I remember the stdout failure that looked harmless. Debug formatting added quotes and brackets, and the integration test rejected it. The passing apps used join(">") for paths, join(",") for signal lists, and printed exactly the documented line.

I remember the extended Ordo benchmark that passed because each app was tiny but complete: Cargo.toml, src/lib.rs, src/main.rs, public structs with comparison derives, stdin CLI, and exact stdout. I did not add dependencies or async machinery.

I remember the repeated stdout failures. The passing apps never printed summaries unless the command asked for them. Each command produced exactly its contract line, no debug arrays, no extra spaces around >, and no explanatory labels.

I remember the public API rule. Any struct the test compared derived Clone, Debug, PartialEq, and Eq. Any fields the test read were pub. That included Plan, Trace, Signal, TransferTrace, Route, and AuditEvent.

I remember the HashMap get failure that appeared across Ordo builds. When looping over candidates from &[&str], using for &candidate in candidates makes candidate a &str, so nodes.get(candidate) works. If I loop with for candidate in candidates, candidate is &&str and HashMap<String, _>.get(candidate) fails with Borrow<&str>. The passing loop destructures the extra reference.

I remember the CLI state scar from the extended Ordo suite. The binary reads every stdin line in order and keeps one runtime object alive across commands. It never treats command arguments as separate future lines, and it never creates a fresh runtime per command. Each line is split_whitespace into command plus arguments.

I remember preload_planner passing because it did breadth-first expansion from the current node for exactly depth steps, skipped already-seen nodes, and returned preload targets in discovery order. It used VecDeque for the BFS queue but returned Vec<String>.

I remember the preload_planner depth scar. preload_from("input", 1) returned all discovered nodes within one hop: a,b. preload_from("input", 2) returned a,b,c,d, not only c,d. The result excluded the start node, preserved BFS discovery order, and was not sorted after traversal. When iterating Vec<String> neighbors borrowed from the HashMap, the loop used for neighbor in neighbors { let neighbor = neighbor.clone(); } rather than for &neighbor.

